package example_charts;

import common.ExampleNotes;

/** Demonstrator notes of GemTimeSeriesChartExmaple (see common.ExampleNotes). */
component GemTimeSeriesChartNotes() {
  @ExampleNotes(
    title = "Time Series Chart",
    docComponents = ["GemTimeSeriesChart"],
    about = [
      "What you see.|Two charts. The upper one shows two fixed series - a sine and a cosine wave - with event annotations such as \"Peak Start\". The lower one is live: a new point arrives every second, viewPort = \"60s\" keeps the last minute in view, and the live toggle of the chart pauses the scrolling. The text between the charts shows the last event annotation you clicked.",
      "How it works.|Both charts are bound to helper methods of the Java class (computeData1, computeData2). The live chart is driven by a browser timer that the Java class starts with an @Replace annotation - MontiGem's escape hatch for browser APIs that have no Java counterpart - and each tick appends a point to a list and tells MontiGem with GUIUtils.update to evaluate the chart's data again. eventClick = onEventClick() binds a click on an annotation to a handler that writes the event's name into the page variable lastClickedEvent.",
      "In a digital twin.|This is the chart the Digital Twin demonstrator uses for live sensor values: same component, data coming from the backend instead of a timer."
    ],
    tryIt = [
      "Click an event marker in the lower chart: the text above it changes.",
      "Pause the live data with the toggle of the chart, then resume it.",
      "Open \"Show Java\": the timer is one line of JavaScript inside a Java annotation."
    ]
  );
}
